# KAZE DRIFT — Update 4: Forza-Style Upgrade Shop You Can SEE, Showroom Car Select, Faster Cars, Touge, Multiplayer

This builds on the existing game, `KAZE_DRIFT_SPEC.md` and the earlier update files (`KAZE_DRIFT_UPDATE_2.md`, `KAZE_DRIFT_UPDATE_3.md`). Where they disagree, **this document wins**. All earlier rules still apply: fictional car and part brands only, no real logos, the quality bar, commit after every step, and the game stays playable after every step.

**Work on your own and do not ask me questions.** When something is unclear, choose what a top racing-game designer would, write it in `DECISIONS.md` with one line of reasoning, and keep going.

**If the repo has an image in `reference/` (or I attach one), it's my visual reference for the upgrade screen. Match its layout, mood and level of detail as closely as you can, using our own art.**

Build in this order. Each part has a "Done when" checklist, so don't move on until it passes:
1. Faster acceleration and a stronger sense of speed (Section 1): it's quick and makes everything else feel better.
2. Showroom car select (Section 2)
3. Upgrade shop with Forza Horizon 6-style structure (Section 3)
4. Visible part installs and the engine bay (Section 4)
5. Akagiri Touge mountain pass (Section 5)
6. Multiplayer (Section 6)
7. Final verification (Section 7)

---

## 1. Make the cars accelerate fast (and feel fast)

Right now acceleration feels slow. Fix the physics first, then the feel.

### 1.1 Physics
- **Drive force must come from a real drivetrain:** wheel force = engine torque(rpm) × gear ratio × final drive × drivetrain efficiency (~0.85) ÷ wheel radius. Each car gets its own torque curve in `cars.json` (peak torque, rpm of peak, redline, rev limiter).
- **Short, sporty gearing** with 5–6 gears, so the car pulls hard in 1st–3rd. Shift time ~80 ms (sequential/race gearbox: 40 ms). Auto-shift up at ~95% of redline and down so the engine stays in its power band. Manual shifting is optional.
- **Remove anything that throttles acceleration artificially.** Delete any "power fades with speed" factor such as `(1 − speed/95)`. Top speed must come from aero drag + gearing only. Reduce rolling resistance to realistic values.
- **Traction control and assists must not choke the car.** TCS allows a little wheelspin and is fully off when drift assist is on or the player is drifting. The launch: holding throttle at a standstill builds revs, and releasing the brake gives a strong launch.
- **Turbo boost** builds with rpm and throttle (lag set by turbo size). Show it on a boost gauge.
- Add a hidden "arcade power" multiplier in settings (default 1.15) as a safety knob, and document it.

### 1.2 Target times (stock cars, flat road, assists default)
| Car | 0–100 km/h | 0–200 km/h | Top speed |
|---|---|---|---|
| Takumi AE (starter) | 6.8 s | — | 200 km/h |
| Kumo Sedan | 6.5 s | — | 210 km/h |
| Sakura 18 | 5.8 s | 19 s | 235 km/h |
| Sento RS | 5.5 s | 18 s | 230 km/h |
| Hoshi GT | 5.2 s | 16 s | 250 km/h |
| Kaze R7 '87 | 5.0 s | 15 s | 255 km/h |
| Sakura S15 | 4.9 s | 15 s | 250 km/h |
| Raiden 34 | 4.3 s | 13 s | 280 km/h |

**Fully upgraded** cars should reach **2.8–3.6 s** to 100 km/h. Write a vitest **acceleration test** that runs every car (stock and fully upgraded) through a scripted full-throttle launch and fails if any time is more than 10% off its target. Show 0–100 and top speed on the car select screen and in the upgrade shop, computed from this simulation, so the numbers are honest.

### 1.3 Feel of speed
- Camera FOV widens with speed (up to +12°). Subtle high-frequency camera shake above ~150 km/h, light motion blur (Medium+), and speed lines or wind particles at very high speed.
- Wind noise and tire roar rise with speed. Each upshift gives a short exhaust "blip" and a camera nudge. Make turbo whoosh and blow-off audible.
- A short "kick" when launching: the camera pulls back slightly and the rear squats.

**Done when:** the acceleration test passes for all cars, and a 20-second Playwright capture shows the speedometer climbing quickly and smoothly through the gears.

