## 3. Upgrade shop: Forza Horizon 6-style structure

Model the **structure and flow** on Forza Horizon 6's "Upgrades & Tuning", in our own visual style with our own fictional part brands. Do not copy Forza's artwork, logos or text.

### 3.1 How FH6 works (copy this behavior)
- Upgrading happens from the car's menu under **Upgrades & Tuning**.
- Parts come in tiers: **Stock → Street → Sport → Race** (some categories only have Stock/Race, some add Rally/Drift).
- Buying a part **instantly updates the car's performance rating (rating number + class letter) and the stat bars**. The buy screen shows the before → after change and the cost first.
- **Conversions** (engine swap, aspiration change like a turbo conversion, drivetrain swap) are done first, because they reset some engine/drivetrain parts. Show a warning when a conversion will remove installed parts.
- **Many tuning sliders are locked until the right Race-tier part is installed:** Race Springs & Dampers unlocks alignment (camber, toe, caster), springs, ride height and damping. Race ARBs unlock anti-roll bar stiffness. Race Brakes unlock brake balance and pressure. A race transmission unlocks gearing. A race differential unlocks diff tuning. Adjustable aero unlocks downforce. Grey out locked sliders with "Install Race Springs to tune".
- A **Mechanical Balance** meter in the tuning menu (FH6 shows one): ~0.55–0.60 is balanced, lower = understeer, higher = loose rear. Show it live as sliders move.
- Every part is owned **per car** (buying a turbo for the S15 doesn't give one to the AE).

### 3.2 Categories and parts (all must exist and change the physics)

**Conversion** (do these first):
- **Engine Swap:** I4 2.0 turbo ("SR-style"), I6 2.6 twin-turbo ("RB-style"), I6 3.0 twin-turbo ("2J-style"), 1.3 twin-rotor ("13B-style"), V6 3.5 ("VQ-style"), V8 5.0, and a joke 1.0 motorcycle engine for the kei/Takumi (screams to 13,000 rpm). Each swap changes weight, weight distribution, torque curve, redline and **sound**.
- **Aspiration:** naturally aspirated → single turbo (small/medium/large) → twin turbo → supercharger (centrifugal or twin-screw), depending on the engine.
- **Drivetrain:** RWD / AWD / FWD swaps where sensible.
- **Body kits:** Stock, Street, Widebody, Drift Missile. A widebody unlocks wider tires.

**Engine** (each with Stock/Street/Sport/Race tiers where it makes sense): Intake · Intake Manifold/Throttle Body · Fuel System · Ignition · Exhaust · Camshaft · Valves · Displacement · Pistons & Compression · Turbo/Supercharger upgrade · Intercooler · Oil & Cooling · Flywheel.
- Make the trade-offs real and explained:
  - Race Exhaust is the best value and changes the engine sound.
  - Race Camshaft adds top-end power but loses low-end torque (harder to modulate in a drift).
  - Displacement is the biggest power gain and the most expensive.
  - Bigger turbos mean more lag.
  - A lighter flywheel revs faster.
  - Oil & Cooling prevents power fade over long sessions.

**Platform & Handling:** Brakes · Springs & Dampers (Street / Sport / Race / Rally / **Drift**) · Front ARB · Rear ARB · Chassis Reinforcement / Roll Cage · Weight Reduction (Street −30 kg, Sport −80 kg, Race −150 kg) · **Angle Kit** (35° → 50° → 60° → 70° steering lock) · Hydraulic Handbrake.

**Drivetrain:** Clutch · Transmission (Street / Sport / Race 6-speed / Race Sequential) · Driveline · Differential (Street / Sport / Race / **2-way drift** / **Welded**).

**Tires & Rims:**
- Compounds: Stock / Street / Sport / Semi-Slick / Race Slick / Rally / Snow / **Drift**.
- Front and rear tire width (several steps).
- Rim style (12+ designs, front and rear can differ), rim size front/rear, track width front/rear.
- Tires show a sidewall with our fictional tire brand lettering and **visible wear** that builds up while drifting.

**Aero & Appearance:** Front Bumper/Splitter · Rear Wing (ducktail, low GT, high GT, swan-neck, adjustable race wing) · Side Skirts · Rear Bumper/Diffuser · Hood (stock/vented/carbon) · Canards · Fender flares · Exhaust tips · Window banner · Underglow. Adjustable race aero unlocks a downforce slider and changes grip and top speed for real.

### 3.3 The screens
- **Upgrade Home:** a grid of big category tiles (Conversion, Engine, Platform & Handling, Drivetrain, Tires & Rims, Aero & Appearance, plus **Tuning** and **Paint & Livery**), each showing how many parts are installed. The car is visible in the 3D garage behind the menu at all times.
- **Category view:** a list of slots (e.g. Engine → Intake, Exhaust, Turbo…), each showing its installed tier. Selecting a slot opens the **tier cards** (Stock / Street / Sport / Race), each with:
  - Fictional brand and part name (e.g. "Tora Works Race Exhaust"), a price, and a "Level 8" lock if gated.
  - A short plain-language effect ("+28 hp, louder, power moves higher in the rev range").
  - **Hover/focus = live preview:** the part appears on the car (Section 4) and the stat bars show ghost before→after segments in green/red. The rating number ticks up or down, and the class badge changes color if the car would change class.
- **Buy & Install confirmation:** cost, the new rating/class, the stat changes, the power-to-weight change and the 0–100 change. "Install" plays the install animation (Section 4).
- **Quick Upgrade:** pick a target class (e.g. "Make it A-class") or a style (Drift / Grip / Balanced), and the game buys the best affordable combination, showing the plan and total cost before you confirm.
- **Build presets:** save, load and rename builds per car. Builds export as the build code from the original spec.
- **Always visible:** money, the car's rating/class, and stat bars along the bottom (Speed, Handling, Acceleration, Launch, Braking, Drift) plus the key specs (hp, kg, 0–100).

### 3.4 Rating and class
- Rating 100–999 is computed from power-to-weight, grip, braking, aero and handling (the same function used everywhere). Classes: **D** 100–400, **C** 401–500, **B** 501–600, **A** 601–700, **S1** 701–800, **S2** 801–900, **R** 901–999.
- Events can restrict class ("B-class touge battle").

**Done when:** every listed part exists, can be bought and removed, changes the physics (checked with a unit test for at least one part per category), changes the rating/class, and shows correct before→after stats.

