## 4. See the parts go in: visible installs and the engine bay

The core feature of this update. **Every** part either has a visible 3D change or a clear visual stand-in, and every install is animated. Build parts procedurally (or from Kenney/our own simple models), sized from each car's bounding box, wheel positions and engine-bay anchor so they fit every car.

### 4.1 Garage set-up
- The car sits on a **two-post hydraulic lift** in our tuning shop (tool walls, tire racks, fluorescent lights, posters for fictional brands, the shop cat).
- **Camera presets** fly smoothly (~0.6 s, ease-in-out) to:
  - **Engine Bay:** the hood opens on a hinge animation and the camera looks down into the bay.
  - **Underside:** the lift raises the car and the camera moves underneath.
  - **Wheel:** close up on the front-left wheel.
  - **Rear:** wing, exhaust and diffuser.
  - **Front:** bumper and splitter.
  - **Interior:** a view through the door window.
  - **Full car:** the default orbit.
- Each slot is tied to a camera preset. Opening a slot moves the camera there automatically.
- An **X-ray toggle** fades the body to a translucent ghost so hidden parts (turbo plumbing, exhaust route, diff, roll cage) can be seen from the outside.

### 4.2 The engine bay (build a real one)
When the hood opens there must be a detailed engine bay, not an empty hole:
- **The engine block matches the installed engine type:** inline-4, inline-6 (long), V6, V8, a compact rotary, and the motorcycle engine. Each has a cam cover with our fictional brand embossed, coil packs/plug wires, belts and pulleys.
- **Visible components, each changing by tier:**
  - **Intake:** a stock airbox with a black plastic duct → a Street drop-in filter → a Sport cone filter → a Race carbon intake with a velocity stack.
  - **Intake manifold/throttle body:** stock cast → polished → a race plenum with a big throttle body.
  - **Turbo:** a turbo housing (snail shape), compressor and turbine, an oil line, and a **blow-off valve**. Size grows with the tier. Twin turbo shows two. A supercharger shows a blower on top (twin-screw) or a belt-driven centrifugal unit.
  - **Intercooler:** a front-mount core visible through the bumper opening, with piping running to the engine (silicone couplers in blue or red on Race).
  - **Exhaust manifold/headers:** stock cast iron → stainless tubular headers (heat-tinted gold/blue on Race).
  - **Fuel system:** a fuel rail and braided lines (red/blue anodized fittings on Race).
  - **Ignition:** coil packs whose color changes with tier.
  - **Oil & Cooling:** an aluminum radiator, an oil cooler at the front, and a catch can.
  - **Chassis:** a strut tower brace on Sport/Race reinforcement.
- **Live engine:** the engine idles with a subtle shake and fan spin. A "**Rev**" button (and holding throttle) revs it, so you can **hear** exhaust, turbo, BOV and intake changes right in the garage.

### 4.3 Under the car and elsewhere
- **Exhaust:** the full route visible from underneath (downpipe → mid pipe → muffler → tips). Tiers change pipe diameter, add a titanium "burnt" finish on Race, and change the tips (single, dual, quad, side exit, bosozoku "takeyari" upswept). Tips are visible from the rear camera.
- **Transmission and clutch:** a gearbox housing behind the engine. Sequential adds a visible shifter in the interior.
- **Driveline/differential:** a driveshaft and diff housing. Race diff gets a finned aluminum cover.
- **Springs & Dampers:** coilovers visible in each wheel well (colored springs by brand and tier). Ride height visibly lowers.
- **Brakes:** calipers and rotors visible through the spokes. Bigger rotors, 4-piston → 6-piston calipers, painted calipers (choose a color), drilled/slotted rotors on Race.
- **Tires and rims:** the wheel is unbolted (lug nuts spin off), slides out and the new one slides on. Tire width visibly changes. Sidewall lettering shows the tire brand. Drift tires have white lettering. Camber visibly tilts the wheel.
- **Weight reduction:** the interior visibly changes: the rear seats disappear (Sport), and carpets and trim disappear for a bare-metal floor (Race). A **roll cage** appears in the cabin, visible through the windows.
- **Angle kit:** the front wheels sweep to full lock to show the new angle, with an angle readout ("62°").
- **Hydraulic handbrake:** a vertical handle appears next to the shifter.
- **Aero and body:** wings, splitters, skirts, canards, flares and hoods bolt on with the animation below.

### 4.4 The install animation (every purchase, ~1.5–2.5 s, skippable)
1. The camera flies to the slot's preset (hood opens, or car lifts, if needed).
2. The old part highlights in red, unbolts (bolt-spin particles, wrench "clank" sounds) and lifts away with a fade.
3. The new part slides into place from a short offset, lands with a "thunk", and the highlight flashes green. A short spark burst for metal parts, a "pssht" for hoses.
4. The stat bars animate to their new values, the rating counter rolls, and if the class changes the badge flips with a sting sound.
5. The camera returns to the category view. A "Hear it" prompt appears for engine/exhaust parts, plus a short auto-rev to show off the new sound.

Allow "Install All" from Quick Upgrade to play a fast montage (0.4 s per part).

### 4.5 Visual-only customization
- Paint and livery from the earlier spec (body, wheels, calipers and window banner colors; finishes: gloss/metallic/pearl/matte/chrome), plus **window decals**: FH6 added painting windows, so support banners on glass.
- Plates with a fictional Japanese-style plate format.

**Done when:** for every slot in Section 3.2, opening it moves the camera to the right place, hovering a tier shows that tier on the car, and buying it plays the install animation with the new part visible afterward. Take a screenshot of each slot before and after install (`screenshots/update-4/parts/`) and look at them all yourself.

